here in the Yaak Valley of northern  Mexico farmers are busy tending their  wheat like most thriving agricultural  communities these farmers benefit from  fertile soil and irrigation but the main  reason that nearby ciudad obregon has  grown into a thriving city and the  breadbasket of Mexico is because of the  research of Norman Borlaug more than 60  years ago he created Hardy plants that  could resist a virulent fungus called we  trust I have seen the misery that comes  from rust epidemics and other foliar  diseases but the rust are unique because  they are carried by the wind her own  distance during the summer seasons  Borlaug's varieties boosted yields and  they helped keep hundreds of millions of  people from starving for that work for  log won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970  and this spring at 94 years old he  journeyed back to ciudad obregon here  where it all started the reason Borlaug  is back is because he's leading a  worldwide effort to combat a new and  dire threat to wheat production a  dangerous new type of stem rust appeared  in Uganda 10 years ago and scientists  around the world are trying to come up  with new varieties of wheat that can  survive it the new fungus has already  spread to Kenya and Ethiopia where it is  now regularly found in the moist  Highlands spores have turned up as far  as Iran and likely will spread even  further east to india and pakistan where  untold numbers of subsistence farmers  grow wheat Ronnie Kaufman of the Borlaug  global rust initiative says an outbreak  there could have terrible consequences  and it will mean the loss of lives and  livelihoods for literally millions of  people if it catches fire in the wrong  place at the wrong time when Kaufman  says catching fire he's talking about  the ability of rust to produce massive  numbers of spores that spread with the  wind these spores take a harsh tool on  susceptible plants infected they become  almost worthless we  shriveled grain that no one buys while  fungicides 